<h2 id="integrating-auth0-for-authentication-and-authorization-optional" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Integrating Auth0 for Authentication and Authorization (Optional)</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#integrating-auth0-for-authentication-and-authorization-optional" aria-label="Link to Integrating Auth0 for Authentication and Authorization (Optional)"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>If you&#x27;re looking to add an extra layer of security and use external identity providers, integrating your ASP.NET Core application with Auth0 is a good option. This allows your users to authenticate using social accounts (like Google, GitHub, etc.) or enterprise identity providers.</p>
